# DESCRIPTION

The system has one clock.
It is monotonic, counts nanoseconds, and nothing changes it; every timestamp the kernel gives and every deadline it takes is a time on it, a `vx_instant`.
The wall clock, the time of day in UTC, is not a second clock but an offset added to this one.

Waiting is as simple.
There is no sleep call and no timer object: a call that waits takes a deadline, the time on the clock at which it gives up, and a program that wants only to pass time waits for something that will not come, until its deadline.
Everything that takes long, a message, an interrupt, a child's end, a doorbell, is waited for in one place, a port, and threads that share memory wait on a futex.

This page describes the clocks, how deadlines work, the two ways to wait, the scheduler as it is now, and what POSIX's time calls become.

@node=clocks title="The clocks"

The monotonic clock is made from the CPU's cycle counter, the TSC on x86_64 and the virtual counter on aarch64, as the count times 10^9 over the counter's frequency, which the kernel calibrates at boot (clock(2)).
It counts from the counter's zero, about when the machine was reset.
`vx_clock_read` returns it.
User code may read the counter itself, without entering the kernel, with `vx_cycles`; `/sys/clock/info`, from sysfs(4), and `vx_clock_info_read` give the frequency to convert it, so a program that times itself with `rdtsc` or `cntvct_el0` never calibrates or guesses.

The wall clock is the monotonic clock plus the kernel's `utc_offset`, which `vx_clock_utc` adds for a caller: UTC in nanoseconds since 1970.
The offset is 0 until something sets it, so until then UTC counts from the counter's zero and dates read as 1970.

It is set once, at boot, from the machine's real-time clock (ADR-0031).
On a PC, bus-acpi(8) reports the CMOS clock, `PNP0B00`; devmgr(8) starts drv-rtc-cmos(3) for it, which reads the clock and tells devmgr the time and when it read it; devmgr, which holds the root Resource the call needs, sets the offset with `clock_set` and says so on the console.
The change is one step; the monotonic clock does not move.

`/sys/clock/now` shows both clocks, and the C library's realtime clocks, file times in fsd(4) and tmpfs(4), and date(1) all read the wall clock through the same offset.

@node=deadlines title="Deadlines and timeouts"

A call that waits takes an absolute deadline on the monotonic clock, never an interval:

- `port_wait`, for a packet (port(2));
- `futex_wait`, for a word to change (futex(2));
- `channel_call`, for a reply (channel(2)).

`VX_INFINITE` is the deadline that never comes, and a deadline already past makes the call a poll.
When the deadline passes first the call returns `VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT`.
Every wait also ends early for a note, with `VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED`, and when the task is being killed, with `VX_ERR_KILLED` (notes(7)).

A deadline is absolute so that a wait cut short by a note and made again keeps the time it was first given, and a program that waits in several steps, a loop, a retry, a call that blocks twice, keeps one limit for all of them.
A program reads the clock once and adds its interval: `vx_clock_read() + 5'000'000'000` is five seconds from now.

`port_wait` also takes a <leeway>, how late it may be woken, so that one timer interrupt can end several waits; a wait is never woken early.

A few calls take an interval instead, because they wait on another's behalf: a pager's deadline is how long a thread that touches a missing page waits for it (pager(2)), and `thread_suspend` gives up on a thread that has not stopped within a second (thread(2)).

The system's own timeouts are the servers' choices.
A connection to a file server is made with a five-second deadline on its `channel_call` (`lib/vx-9p/ring.c`); procfs(4) gives each call it makes to save a crash directory five seconds, since the process that faulted may be the one it waits on.
A 9P request itself has no deadline (see BUGS).

@node=futexes title="Futexes"

A futex is a 32-bit word in memory that threads wait on: the building block of locks and condition variables (futex(2)).
`futex_wait` blocks while the word holds the value the caller expects, until a `futex_wake` or its deadline; the word is checked and the thread queued under one lock, so a wake after the check is never missed, and a word changed before it returns at once.
`futex_wake` wakes as many waiters as asked, the most recent first.

A futex is keyed by the word's physical address, so the same word mapped into two tasks through a shared VMO is one futex.
The C library's locks, and its sleeps, wait on futexes.

@node=ports title="Ports: waiting on several things"

A port is a queue of packets, and a thread that waits for anything but a lock waits on a port, with one call, `port_wait`, however many things it waits for (port(2)).
Each source is bound to the port with `port_bind`, naming a trigger and a key of the program's choosing; when the trigger holds, the port gets one packet with that key, and the binding is gone.

| Source | Fires when |
| channel end | a message is queued, or the other end has gone |
| ring end | its doorbell reaches a value, or the other end has gone |
| counter | it reaches a value |
| task | it has ended |
| Irq | its interrupt has fired |
| DmaDomain | its device has faulted more than a number of times |

and exceptions and a pager's requests reach ports bound by `exception_bind` and `pager_create`.

A binding is one-shot, and fires at once if its trigger already holds when it is made, so nothing is lost between looking at a source and binding it.
A program's loop is therefore: bind each source, wait, handle each packet, and bind its source again.
`port_post` puts a packet of the program's own on the port, for a thread to wake another.
Several threads may wait on one port; each packet wakes one of them.

A counter is a timeline that only goes up (counter(2)): a producer raises it as work completes, and a waiter binds it with the value it waits for.
A ring's doorbells are counters, so waiting for a ring is waiting on a port too (rings(7)).

@node=sched title="The scheduler"

The scheduler is the first one the design sketches, and simple (`kernel/sched/sched.c`).
Every CPU serves one shared queue of ready threads, round robin, and runs a thread for at most 10 ms while others wait.
Each CPU keeps the threads that blocked on it with a deadline in a queue of its own, earliest first, and arms its timer for the first of them or the end of the running slice, whichever comes sooner; a CPU with nothing to run sleeps with no timer armed unless a sleeper needs one, and a thread made ready elsewhere reaches it as an interrupt.

Every thread runs as `interactive`, the intent channel messages carry as `sender_intent`; the calls that are to make scheduling contexts and reserve cores are reserved, and return `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` (sched(2)).

@node=posix title="POSIX's time calls"

The C library (`ports/musl/vx/start.c`) makes POSIX's time calls from these:

| Call | Becomes |
| `clock_gettime`, `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` and the rest | the monotonic clock |
| `clock_gettime`, `CLOCK_REALTIME` and `CLOCK_TAI` | the wall clock |
| `gettimeofday`, `time` | the wall clock, through `clock_gettime` |
| `clock_getres` | 1 ns, for every clock |
| `nanosleep`, `clock_nanosleep` | a `futex_wait` with a deadline |
| `poll`, `select`, `ppoll`, `pselect` | a `port_wait` with a deadline |
| `futex`, under the C library's locks | `futex_wait` and `futex_wake` |
| `sched_yield` | nothing |

A sleep or a poll that a signal cuts short, and that is made again for `SA_RESTART`, keeps its first deadline.
A sleep to an absolute time of day is turned into a deadline on the monotonic clock when the call is made.

sleep(1) is a `nanosleep`; date(1) reads the wall clock.

# EXAMPLES

The wall clock's test, `tests/user/rtctest.rc`, run by rc(1) in the POSIX namespace under QEMU with its clock started at 2030-01-02T03:04:05.
Since nothing orders a service after the clock is set, it waits for the year to stop being 1970:

```rc
tries=0
while(~ `{date -u +%Y} 1970 && ! ~ $tries 300) {
  sleep 0.1
  tries=`{expr $tries + 1}
}
echo rtctest: date `{date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H'}
touch /tmp/stamped
echo rtctest: file `{ls -l /tmp/stamped}
```

On the console, the driver and devmgr setting the clock, then the test:

```text
drv-rtc-cmos: the clock says 1893553446 s since 1970
devmgr: the wall clock is 2030-01-02 03:04:06 UTC, from /boot/bin/drv-rtc-cmos
svcd: started rtctest (task 15)
rtctest: date 2030-01-02 03
rtctest: file -rw-r--r-- 1 0 0 0 Jan 02 03:04 /tmp/stamped
```

