# BUGS

The wall clock is x86_64's alone: QEMU's aarch64 tables list no real-time clock and there is no PL031 driver, so on aarch64 dates read as 1970.
It is set once, in one step, never slewed, never kept by NTP and never written back to the real-time clock; a sleep already armed against a time of day keeps its old deadline when the clock is set.
Nothing orders a service after the clock is set, so one that starts first stamps times that read as 1970, which is why the test above waits.

`clock_read` always enters the kernel: the shared page the design reads the clock from without a call does not exist yet, nor does the counters' (counter(2)).
The monotonic clock counts from the counter's reset, not from the kernel's boot.

A 9P request has no deadline and cannot be cancelled: a read a server holds, such as ping(8)'s with no reply, waits until it is answered.
`Tflush` comes with the pipelined 9P client in M6 step 6d.

A thread that dies holding a lock built on a futex leaves its waiters asleep; robust futexes come in M6 step 6d, with threads in the C library, whose `clone`, and so `pthread_create`, fails until then.
`port_cancel` is reserved: a binding cannot be withdrawn before it fires except by destroying its source.

None of the design's scheduling exists (`docs/01-kernel-ipc.md` §8): no intents but `interactive`, no budgets, no realtime admission, no donation of a caller's time through `channel_call`, no core reservations and no placement on cores of different kinds.
Scheduling contexts come in M6 step 6d.

The C library has no `alarm`, `setitimer`, POSIX timers, `times` or `clock_settime`: each fails with `ENOSYS`, after a line on the debug console, so nothing ever sends `SIGALRM`, time(1) prints no true figures, and date(1) cannot set the date.
Its CPU-time clocks are the monotonic clock, and `sched_yield` yields nothing.
